WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:08.805
We're here in the Arthur M. Sackler Museum of the Harvard Art Museums and

00:00:08.805 --> 00:00:13.750
we're here with Doctor Robert Mowry, who's Alan J. Dworsky Curator of

00:00:13.750 --> 00:00:16.400
Chinese Art and has been here at Harvard since 1986.

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:20.750
One of the extraordinary features of Harvard's Chinese collection are a

00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:23.190
series of bronzes from the Shang dynasty.

00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:26.200
Perhaps you could just say a few words about the technology of

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:27.480
making these bronzes.

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:28.080
Sure.

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:32.119
These bronzes are made using a very complex method that was not used any

00:00:32.119 --> 00:00:36.240
place else in the world, most any time, maybe until the 20th century,

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:37.520
maybe, but not before that.

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:39.610
It's called the piece mold technique.

00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:44.350
And the way they did it, they first made a model of the vessel in clay,

00:00:44.350 --> 00:00:48.600
which they fired to give it strength, durability, and a very hard surface.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:54.600
Then they would take small pieces of clay and put them over sections of the

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:57.200
vessel to make the mold itself.

00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:58.840
And that's why it's called piece mold.

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:01.010
It's in numerous different pieces.

00:01:01.010 --> 00:01:04.379
After they have made impressions from the model, decoration was

00:01:04.379 --> 00:01:06.070
carved into the model.

00:01:06.070 --> 00:01:09.220
Never forget that one of the glories of these vessels is that everything

00:01:09.220 --> 00:01:12.370
that you see on the surface was integrally cast

00:01:12.370 --> 00:01:14.190
with the vessel itself.

00:01:14.190 --> 00:01:17.630
There is very little cold working, that is post casting, chasing,

00:01:17.630 --> 00:01:19.230
chiseling, and such.

00:01:19.230 --> 00:01:23.840
It is integrally cast so that that decoration, for the most part, was

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:27.910
incised, engraved into the model, so that when you put the piece of clay

00:01:27.910 --> 00:01:31.900
against the model and then pull it off, it would have part of the shape

00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:34.250
of the vessel and part of the decoration.

00:01:34.250 --> 00:01:37.880
Of course, those being clay and the bronze, when its mold's going to go

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:42.650
in, is going to be very hot means that the pieces have to be fired.

00:01:42.650 --> 00:01:45.190
This is a difficult part because whenever you fire clay

00:01:45.190 --> 00:01:46.670
it's going to shrink.

00:01:46.670 --> 00:01:50.350
And so then you have to have pieces that even though they have shrunken

00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:52.250
still fit together perfectly.

00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:53.650
And then you would bind them together.

00:01:53.650 --> 00:01:55.780
You have an inner mold, otherwise you're going to have a completely

00:01:55.780 --> 00:01:57.200
solid vessel.

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:01.760
Little pieces of bronze called chaplets that hold the interior mold

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:05.380
away from the pieces of the outer mold when it's all set.

00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:08.009
You put the molten bronze in.

00:02:08.009 --> 00:02:10.250
Of course, the whole thing is upside down.

00:02:10.250 --> 00:02:13.420
The reason being when it's upside down, you don't want any bubbles.

00:02:13.420 --> 00:02:15.710
There are going to be bubbles in that molten bronze.

00:02:15.710 --> 00:02:19.150
When they rise to the top, you want them on the bottom of the legs, where

00:02:19.150 --> 00:02:20.440
no one's going to see them.

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:24.670
So it's a very complex process, but it gives superior casting results.

